  "reference_answer": "Hand selection techniques allow users to select virtual objects using their hand. Initially, virtual hand tracking uses a direct one-to-one mapping of the user's physical hand motion to the virtual hand, which is intuitive but tiring for distant objects. To overcome this, travel techniques like Go-Go and Prism allow for more efficient movement, mapping small real movements to larger virtual movements or controlling speed. Separately, intent-driven selection is a grabbing technique that uses finger postures and gestures, often employing proximity spheres for progressive refinement. These two approaches can be combined, allowing the user to travel to an object and then use gestures to select it.",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "one-to-one mapping",
      "keywords": ["one-to-one mapping", "physical hand motion"]
    },
